# Rust

The root `Cargo.toml` lints and `clippy.toml` enforce the mechanical parts. CI
runs clippy with `-D warnings` on every OS. Every crate sets
`[lints] workspace = true`.

## Errors and panics

- Library crates return `Result` with the crate's own error enum, built with
  `thiserror`. No `anyhow` or `Box<dyn Error>` in library APIs.
- Bad input is an error, never a panic. `unwrap()` is denied outside tests. Use
  `expect("...")` only for a true invariant, and say the invariant in the message.
- Error variants carry what a developer needs to debug: the codec, the field,
  the bad value, the OS status code.
- No `todo!`, `unimplemented!`, `dbg!`, `println!`, or `std::process::exit` in
  committed code. Log with `tracing`.

## Lint exceptions

Use `#[expect(lint, reason = "...")]`, never `#[allow]`. An `expect` fails once
the exception is no longer needed.

```rust
// ❌
#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]

// ✅
#[expect(clippy::too_many_arguments, reason = "mirrors VTDecompressionSessionCreate")]
```

## Unsafe

- Software crates set `#![deny(unsafe_code)]`. The only exception is a `simd`
  module. It opts in with `#![expect(unsafe_code, reason = "...")]`, and it is
  tested against a scalar version.
- Native crates use `unsafe` only around the FFI call. Every block has a
  `// SAFETY:` comment saying why it is sound. Every `unsafe fn` has a
  `# Safety` doc section. Public APIs are safe.

## API shape

- Keep items private or `pub(crate)` until another crate needs them. Re-export
  the public API from `lib.rs`.
- Types with invariants have private fields and a constructor that checks them.
- Decoders and encoders are `Send`, so a worker thread can own them.
- Match workspace enums exhaustively. No `_ =>` arm on codec, pixel format, or
  error enums. No `#[non_exhaustive]` on workspace-internal enums. The compiler
  should list every place a new variant needs handling.
- Convert integers that come from input with `try_from` or checked math. Use
  `as` only when the range is already proven.

## Modules

- Directory modules use `mod.rs`. `clippy::self_named_module_files` enforces it.
- One concept per file. Split a file when it holds two things a reader would
  look for separately.
- Put the OS gate on the whole crate (`#![cfg(...)]` in `lib.rs`), not on scattered
  items.

## Comments

- A comment says what the code can't show: a constraint, an invariant, a spec
  clause, an OS quirk.
- Don't narrate the next line, explain how the code came to be, or argue that a
  change is correct. That goes in the commit message.
- Public items get a short `///` doc. Doc links must resolve, because CI runs
  `cargo doc` with `-D warnings`.
